WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.750
RETO MEIER: Google
Cloud Next 2017

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:04.962
hosted a litany of talks
detailing the future of cloud.

00:00:04.962 --> 00:00:07.420
If you didn't get a chance to
catch the session "Developing

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.850
for Speed Minimizing
App Latency at Scale,"

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.310
then stay tuned because
here is the recap.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:13.672
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.640
You're a good developer
so you already

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.160
know the importance
of having a fast app.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.950
Lower latency correlates to an
increase in user engagement,

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:26.240
sales, and user satisfaction.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.600
COLT MCANLIS: Now latency is
the delay between a request

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:29.830
and its response.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.530
And application
latency, specifically,

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:35.440
is how this delay manifests
and affects the end

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.090
user, which is important.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:39.430
As little as a 100
milliseconds increase in page

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:43.304
latency can cause around
a 1% drop in revenue.

00:00:43.304 --> 00:00:45.220
RETO MEIER: Which means
that the ultimate goal

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.260
is to manage latency
in a holistic manner

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.421
to maximize user satisfaction.

00:00:49.421 --> 00:00:51.670
You know you've done a good
job when performance plays

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:53.980
no part in a user's experience.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.147
They simply don't notice it.

00:00:55.147 --> 00:00:56.563
COLT MCANLIS: A
good rule of thumb

00:00:56.563 --> 00:00:58.240
is to focus your
attention on making

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.370
your most common
operations the fastest

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:03.310
and aligning behavior
with user expectations.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:06.070
See, users intuitively
expect some operations

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:09.130
will take a bit longer, for
example, creating a new file

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:10.060
or saving one.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.460
Now even for operation
when that's not the case,

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.130
or where the delay is likely
longer than they're expecting,

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.980
you can manage those
expectations by telling users

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.961
the operation may take a while.

00:01:19.961 --> 00:01:21.460
RETO MEIER: From a
user perspective,

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.410
the perceived
latency is the time

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:26.320
spent interacting with the
UI, the actual latency,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.936
and the difference between
their expectations.

00:01:28.936 --> 00:01:30.310
There are some
great ways to make

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.280
your app feel like it's
loading faster without actually

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.740
decreasing its actual latency--

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.740
provide instant feedback,
animate straight transitions,

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.700
and cache your UI components so
that they appear immediately.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.280
Showing your UI even if it
can't be interacted with

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.040
will decrease the
perceived latency.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.560
COLT MCANLIS: If you want to
improve the actual latency,

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.480
the first step is monitoring it.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.060
Google Analytics lets
you specify user timings

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.310
to investigate the
distribution of latency values

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.040
for different actions and
the difference in values

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.890
across different demographics,
including regional differences.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.980
Another great tool
is Stackdriver.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.020
Using tracing, you can view
latency numbers in real time,

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.110
plot it on a scatter
graph, and dig deeper

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:12.970
by selecting individual traces.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:14.960
Stackdriver also
produces reports,

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.290
including sampling traces
at different percentiles,

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.030
for you to look at.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.400
RETO MEIER: When you identify
a high latency function

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.660
or request type
that's CPU bound,

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:26.020
then profiling lets
you dig deeper.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.360
Once you've identified
potential latency issues,

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:29.722
it's time to mitigate them.

00:02:29.722 --> 00:02:31.180
COLT MCANLIS: One
great approach is

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.310
to find out where
your users are and put

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.740
your compute and storage
resources near them.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.200
To help, use the
Google Cloud CDN,

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.440
or content delivery network,
to take advantage of Google's

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.930
globally distributed
edge points of presence

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:48.970
to catch HTTP load balanced
content close to your users.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:51.550
RETO MEIER: Above all else,
focus on user satisfaction

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.830
with a holistic approach
that includes both front end

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:54.997
and back end.

00:02:54.997 --> 00:02:56.830
If you're interested
in more ideas about how

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:59.950
to use Google CDN and details
on performing a performance

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:03.730
analysis on your apps, check
out the full talk on YouTube.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:06.360
And if you want more recaps
on great Next content,

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.360
make sure to check out
the rest of our playlist.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.780
And don't forget the Google
Cloud Summit coming soon

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:11.720
to a city near you.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:13.870
